raw_text:
My intellectual powers, far from bringing me closer to them, were becoming the very tools of my alienation. Every word I mastered, every concept I grasped, served only to draw clearer the lines of my exclusion, to etch more deeply the chasm between my being and theirs. I had boasted that I improved more rapidly than the Arabian, that I comprehended and could imitate almost every word that was spoken; yet this swift acquisition of language, this gateway to human thought, opened not to acceptance but to a profound understanding of my own singular, abhorrent condition. It was as if I had been granted the gift of sight, only to perceive with dreadful clarity the walls of my prison.

While I improved in speech, I also learned the science of letters as it was taught to the stranger, and this indeed opened before me a wide field for wonder and delight, though a delight ever mingled with an acute, piercing sorrow. The book from which Felix instructed Safie was Volney’s *Ruins of Empires*. I absorbed its contents with an avidity that knew no bounds, for Felix, in reading it, gave very minute explanations, elucidating the profound meaning and consequence of each historical epoch and social institution. He had chosen this work, he said, because the declamatory style was framed in imitation of the Eastern authors, a manner of speaking that he deemed suitable for his pupil, but for me, it was a revelation, a panoramic vista of the human drama unfolding across centuries and continents.

Through this work, I obtained a cursory knowledge of history and a view of the several empires at present existing in the world;

